Article 5 of the AI Act prohibits AI practices that:  

(a) place on the market, put into service or use an AI system that employs subliminal, purposefully manipulative or deceptive techniques beyond a person’s consciousness, materially distorting a person’s or group’s behaviour and impairing their ability to make an informed decision, thereby causing or likely causing significant harm;  

(b) place on the market, put into service or use an AI system that exploits vulnerabilities of a natural person or specific group (e.g., due to age, disability, or social/economic situation) to materially distort behaviour and cause or likely cause significant harm;  

(c) place on the market, put into service or use AI systems for the evaluation or classification of natural persons or groups over a certain period based on their social behaviour or known, inferred or predicted personal characteristics, where such practices are intended to materially distort behaviour and cause or likely cause significant harm. [5]

Processing is lawful only if at least one of the following applies:  

(a) the data subject has given consent for one or more specific purposes;  
(b) processing is necessary for the performance of a contract to which the data subject is party or to take steps at the data subject’s request before entering into a contract;  
(c) processing is necessary for compliance with a legal obligation to which the controller is subject;  
(d) processing is necessary to protect the vital interests of the data subject or another natural person;  
(e) processing is necessary for the performance of a task carried out in the public interest or in the exercise of official authority vested in the controller;  
(f) processing is necessary for the purposes of the legitimate interests pursued by the controller or a third party, except where such interests are overridden by the data subject’s interests or fundamental rights and freedoms (the point does not apply to public authorities in the performance of their tasks)【4†L1-L7】.

The Data Protection Commission may 

* issue opinions on matters related to the protection of personal data to the Oireachtas, Government, public authorities, bodies or the public, either on its own initiative or on request [1];  
* advise on and monitor the application of data‑protection law in the State to protect fundamental rights in relation to personal‑data processing [4];  
* decide, after an inquiry of its own volition (under Chapter 2, Chapter 3 or in respect of a complaint), whether an infringement by a controller or processor has occurred and, if so, whether to exercise a corrective power and which corrective power to apply [5][7][8];  
* exercise the corrective powers it decides on, which may include orders or other measures against the controller or processor [5][7][8];  
* when it restricts a data‑subject’s rights or obligations, notify the data subject of the restriction, the reasons (unless prejudice would result), the right or obligation affected, the extent of the restriction and the statutory right to lodge a complaint [2][6].
